{{History of literature by era}}
'''Georgian literature''' ({{lang-ka|ქართული ლიტერატურა}}) refers to a long literary heritage, with some of the oldest surviving texts in [[Georgian language]] dating back to the 5th century. A golden age of Georgian literature flourished under the unified kingdom of [[David IV]] in the 11th century. However, political fragmentation and foreign invasions following a peak in literary tradition during the reign of [[Tamar of Georgia|Queen Tamar]] (1184–1213 CE) led to a cultural decline.

{{short description|Sudden change of rhythm or bass line}}
{{Redirect|Pop drop| Tootsie Pop Drops | Tootsie Pop}}{{Listen
| filename     = Disparition - Neukrk - Glass Tiger.ogg
| title        = "Glass Tiger"
| description  = A track by Disparition that incorporates a beat drop
}}
[[file:Disparition - Neukrk - Glass Tiger.svg|alt=audio spectrogram|thumb|In this [[spectrogram]] of "Glass Tiger", the buildup and drop are visible leading up to 2:05]]

A '''drop''' or '''beat drop''' in music, made popular by [[electronic dance music]] (EDM) styles, is a point in a [[Song|music track]] where a sudden change of [[rhythm]] or [[bass line]] occurs, which is preceded by a build-up section and [[Break (music)|break]].<ref>{{Cite book|last=Young|first=Rob|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H9K7ZSvjFp4C&q=%22bass%20drop%22%20dubstep&pg=PA103|title=La guida alla musica moderna di Wire|date=2010|publisher=Isbn Edizioni|isbn=978-88-7638-180-5|language=it}}</ref> The drop is the loudest portion of an EDM song. 

Originating from [[disco]] and 1970s rock, drops are found in genres such as [[Electronic dance music|EDM]], [[EDM trap music|trap]], [[hip-hop]], [[K-pop]] and [[Country music|country]]. With the aid of music production applications, drops can vary in instrumentation and sound. Electronic instruments and tools for making drops include [[synthesizer]]s, vocal [[Sampling (music)|samples]], a drum beat, and basslines.

Certain drops can include a "beat-up" (so-named because it is a point where the volume of the foundational [[kick drum]] beat is increased, after it has been faded down during a [[Break (music)|break]] or buildup) and "climax" (a single, striking drop done late in the track). There are also types of drops which deviate from the standard, such as "anti-drops" (songs in which the chorus is more minimal than the build-up) and consecutive "superseding-drops".

